!!Seleucia!! ([[ /sɪˈljuːʃə/ | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help%3AIPA%2FEnglish ]]; Ancient Greek: Σελεύκεια), also known as !!Seleucia-on-Tigris!!, !!Seleucia on the Tigris!! or !!Seleucia ad Tigrim!!, was a major [[ Mesopotamian | mesopotamia ]] city on the west bank of the [[ Tigris River ]], within the present-day [[ Baghdad Governorate ]] in [[ Iraq ]]. [[ Seleucus I Nicator ]] founded it around 305 BC as the first capital of the [[ Seleucid Empire ]], and losing that role did not diminish it: after the imperial capital relocated to [[ Antioch ]], Seleucia remained an important center of trade and [[ Hellenistic | hellenistic-period ]] culture, and it went on flourishing under [[ Parthian | parthian-empire ]] rule beginning in 141 BC. Ancient texts claim that it reached a population of 600,000.

The end came from Rome. Seleucia was destroyed in 165 AD by [[ Roman | roman-empire ]] general [[ Avidius Cassius ]] and gradually faded into obscurity in the subsequent centuries. The site was rediscovered in the 1920s by archaeologists.

== Names

Seleucia (Ancient Greek: Σελεύκεια, //Seleúkeia//) is named for [[ Seleucus I Nicator ]], who enlarged an earlier settlement and made it the capital of [[ his empire | seleucid-empire ]] around 305 BC. It was the largest and most important of the [[ many cities to bear its name | seleucia-disambiguation ]], and to tell it from the others it is sometimes called Seleucia-on-Tigris or Seleucia on the Tigris (Latin: //Seleucia ad Tigridem//), after [[ its river | tigris-river ]].

The city answered to other names in other tongues. Texts from the [[ Church of the East ]]'s synods referred to it as //Salīq// (Syriac: ܣܠܝܩ)[[ ^1 ]] or some times //Māḥôzē// (Syriac: ܡܚܘܙ̈ܐ) when the metropolis of [[ Seleucia-Ctesiphon ]] was meant. The [[ Sassanids ]] named the eastern city [[ Veh-Ardashir ]] (Persian: ویه‌اردشیر); [[ Arabs ]] called it Bahurasīr.

== History



== Seleucid Empire

The year the city began is argued over. A foundation date of 300 BC was proposed by Auguste Bouch-Leclerq in 1914;[[ ^2 ]] other scholars proposed 306 BC and 312 BC.[[ ^3 ]][[ ^4 ]] Who founded it, and why, is not in doubt: [[ Seleucus I Nicator ]], one of the [[ Diadochi ]] successors of [[ Alexander the Great ]] who divided his empire among themselves after Alexander's death, established Seleucia as the first capital of the [[ Seleucid Empire ]]. Although Seleucus soon moved his main capital to [[ Antioch ]], in northern [[ Syria | syria-region ]], the city he left behind became an important center of trade, Hellenistic culture, and regional government under the Seleucids.

A metropolis needed people, and Seleucus took them from [[ Babylon ]]. Almost all of that city's inhabitants were forced to leave and resettle in Seleucia, except the local temple priests and supporting workers. A tablet dated 275 BC states that the inhabitants of Babylon were transported to Seleucia, where a palace and a temple (Esagila) were built. The site itself was chosen for traffic: standing at the confluence of the Tigris River with a major canal from the [[ Euphrates ]], Seleucia was placed to receive traffic from both great waterways.

During the 3rd and 2nd centuries BC, it was one of the great [[ Hellenistic ]] cities, comparable to [[ Alexandria ]] in [[ Egypt | ptolemaic-egypt ]], and greater than Syrian [[ Antioch ]]. Excavations indicate that the walls of the city enclosed an area of at least 550 hectares (1,400 acres), equivalent to a square roughly 1.5 miles (2.5 kilometers) on a side. Based on this size, the population has been estimated to number over 100,000 initially and probably more later, in a surrounding region that might have supported half a million people.[[ ^5 ]]

Who lived there shows up in the vocabulary of the sources. Polybius (5,52ff) uses the Macedonian //[[ peliganes ]]// for the council of Seleucia, which implies a Macedonian colony, consistent with its rise to prominence under Seleucus I; Pausanias ([[ 1,16 | https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/cgi-bin/ptext?lookup=Paus.+1.16.3 ]]) records that Seleucus also settled [[ Babylonians | babylonia ]] there. Archaeological finds support the presence of a large population not of Greek culture.

In 141 BC, the Parthians under [[ Mithridates I | mithridates-i-of-parthia ]] conquered the city, and Seleucia became the western capital of the [[ Parthian Empire ]]. Conquest did not change its character. [[ Tacitus ]] described its walls, and mentioned that it was, even under Parthian rule, a fully Hellenistic city. Ancient texts claim that the city had 600,000 inhabitants, and was ruled by a senate of 300 people. It was clearly one of the largest cities in the Western world; only Rome, [[ Alexandria ]], and possibly [[ Antioch ]] were more populous.

In 55 BC, a battle fought near Seleucia was crucial in establishing dynastic succession of the [[ Arsacid ]] kings. In this battle between the reigning [[ Mithridates III | mithridates-iii-of-parthia ]] (supported by a Roman army of [[ Aulus Gabinius ]], governor of Syria) and the previously deposed [[ Orodes II ]], the reigning monarch was defeated, allowing Orodes to re-establish himself as king. In 41 BC, Seleucia was the scene of a massacre of around 5,000 Babylonian Jewish refugees ([[ Josephus ]], [[ //Ant.// | antiquities-of-the-jews ]] xviii. 9, § 9).[[ ^6 ]]

In 117 AD, Seleucia was burned down by the Roman emperor [[ Trajan ]] during his conquest of Mesopotamia, but the following year it was ceded back to the Parthians by Trajan's successor, [[ Hadrian ]], then rebuilt in the [[ Parthian style ]]. The rebuilt city lasted less than fifty years. It was completely destroyed by the [[ Roman | roman-empire ]] general [[ Avidius Cassius ]] in 165.[[ ^7 ]]

== Sasanian rule

Over sixty years later a new city, [[ Veh-Ardashir ]], was built across the river by [[ Persian | persia ]] emperor [[ Ardashir I ]]. This new city was long believed to be located at Seleucia but was shown by Italian excavations to be a fresh construction, separate from Seleucia and [[ Ctesiphon ]].

What made the place matter now was the church. There were active [[ Christian | christianity ]] churches in Mesopotamia from the 1st century onwards, and in the 3rd or 4th century Seleucia became an important centre. Following the edict of toleration by the Persian [[ Sasanian | sasanian-empire ]] King [[ Yazdegerd I ]], which for the time being brought an end to the [[ persecution of Christians ]] that had lasted for 70 years, the remaining Christians set about reorganizing and strengthening the church.

The [[ Council of Seleucia-Ctesiphon ]] (The Synod of Mar Isaac) met in 410 AD under the presidency of [[ Mar Isaac | mar-isaac-bishop ]], the [[ bishop of Seleucia-Ctesiphon ]]. Its most important decision, which had a very far reaching effect on the life of the church, was to declare the [[ bishop of Seleucia-Ctesiphon | list-of-patriarchs-of-the-church-of-the-east ]] the [[ primate | primate-bishop ]] of the [[ Church of the East ]]; in recognition of this pre-eminence he was given the title '[[ Catholicos ]]'. The Synod confirmed Mar Isaac as Catholicos and Archbishop of all the Orient, declared its adherence to the decision of the [[ Council of Nicaea | first-council-of-nicaea ]] and subscribed to the [[ Nicene Creed ]]. The Canons of the Synod leave no doubt as to the authority of the great Metropolitan, the Catholicos of Seleucia-Ctesiphon: without his approval, no election of bishop would be valid.

The toleration did not hold. Towards the end of the reign of Yazdegerd I, the [[ Christians in the Sasanian Empire | christianity-in-the-sasanian-empire ]] were again persecuted in 420. Dadyeshu was elected Catholicos in 421 and himself suffered during the persecution and was imprisoned. When he was released he resigned and left Seleucia, but the church refused to accept the resignation, and there followed the Synod of Dadyeshu, which met in 424 in Markabta of the Arabs under the presidency of Mar Dadyeshu. It proved to be one of the most significant of all Persian synods. The first synod of Isaac in 410 had decided that the Catholicos of Seleucia-Ctesiphon be supreme among the bishops of the East; the Synod of Dadyeshu decided that the Catholicos should be the sole head of the Church of the East and that no ecclesiastical authority should be acknowledged above him. For the first time, this synod referred to the Catholicos as Patriarch and held that their Catholicos was answerable to God alone. This had some effect in reassuring the Sasanian monarchy that the Persian Christians were not influenced by the Roman enemy.

The city eventually faded into obscurity and was swallowed by the desert sands, probably abandoned after the Tigris shifted its course.

== Archaeology

The archaeologists who found Seleucia again in the 1920s were looking for somewhere else — [[ Opis ]].[[ ^8 ]]

Beginning in 1927, [[ University of Michigan ]] professors Leroy Waterman (1927–1932) and Clark Hopkins (1936–1937) oversaw excavations for the [[ Kelsey Museum of Archaeology ]] on behalf of the American School of Oriental Research of Baghdad with funds supplied by the [[ Toledo Museum of Art ]] and the [[ Cleveland Museum of Art ]].[[ ^9 ]][[ ^10 ]][[ ^11 ]] Finds included many coins, mostly bronze, salt receipts with the name of Seleucia, a blue glazed incense burner, now in the Baghdad museum, a stele inscribed in Greek, numerous beads, metal objects including weights and surgeons instruments, and one pre-Sargonic brick.[[ ^12 ]]

From 1964 to 1968 and then between 1985 and 1989, an Italian mission from the [[ University of Turin ]] directed by Antonio Invernizzi and Giorgio Gullini excavated at the site. They found a Seleucid archive building with about 30,000 seal impressions, all in a fully Greek style.[[ ^13 ]][[ ^14 ]][[ ^15 ]]

One piece is far older than the city around it. In an outer wall of the Parthian period, a reused [[ brick ]] dated by stamp to 821 BC, during the [[ Neo-Assyrian ]] period.

[[ ^1 ]]: Thomas A. Carlson et al., “Seleucia-Ctesiphon — ܣܠܝܩ ܘܩܛܝܣܦܘܢ ” in The Syriac Gazetteer last modified May 25, 2016, https://syriaca.org/place/2615.

[[ ^2 ]]: Auguste Bouch-Leclerq, "Histoire des Se'leucides II", Paris, 1914

[[ ^3 ]]: Karl Julius Beloch, "Griechische Geschichte IV i", Berlin, 1923

